<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutare</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Umtali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutare, called Umtali until 1982, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Founded in the 1890s and moved to its present site to meet the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beira">Beira</a>, which reached it in 1898, it became the gateway between the Rhodesian interior and the coast. It is the capital of Manicaland Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutare.jpg" alt="Mutare" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Seabifar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mutare_aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mutare lies near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, about 290 kilometres from the Mozambican port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beira">Beira</a>, which has earned it the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s Gateway to the Sea. The town lies north of the Bvumba Mountains and south of the Imbeza Valley, and it is also called the Gateway to the Eastern Highlands. Christmas Pass, a winding road with views of the highlands, leads into the city from the west and was named by colonial pioneers who camped at its foot on Christmas Day 1890. Many Zimbabweans call the city Kumakomoyo, place of many mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region had a long history of trading caravans passing through on the way between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> and inland settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>. The British archaeologist E. M. Andrews found a large hoard of soapstone carvings, jewellery, weapons and other objects near Mutare early in the twentieth century; they were donated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1905. The area was the site of Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutasa">Mutasa</a>'s kraal. In 1890 A. R. Coquhoun was given concessionary rights and Fort Umtali was established between the Tsambe and Mutare Rivers. The name Mutare is said to derive from utare, meaning iron or possibly gold, probably given to the river after gold was found in the Penhalonga valley.</p><p>In 1891 the settlement was moved to a site now known as Old Mutare, about 14 kilometres north of the city centre. The construction of the railway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beira">Beira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> led to a third move, closer to the line, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> paid compensation to the townspeople for the cost. A tramway carried passengers from the railway station up to the Umtali Club from 26 January 1897 until 23 May 1921. The town was proclaimed a municipality on 11 June 1914 and was granted city status in 1971. The name was officially changed from Umtali to Mutare in 1982. The population was 46,000 in 1965, 69,621 in 1982 and 131,367 in 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mutare is the capital of Manicaland Province, a centre of trade on the Beira-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> railway, with road and pipeline links between the coast and the interior. The population is predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>, most of them speaking the Manyika dialect. Industries include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, forestry, farming and food processing, and gold is mined in the area. Its attractions include the Mutare Museum, the National Gallery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> and Murahwa Hill, known for its rock paintings and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> village.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutare” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With sovereign rights to tax, grant land and issue its own stamps and currency, the company built Beira into the main port for the landlocked Rhodesias, linked by the Beira railway, which reached <b>Umtali</b> in 1898 and Salisbury in 1899.</blockquote><a href="/state/mozambique-company">Mozambique Company</a></li></ul></section>
